7. Exception Chaining Using getCause()
Exception chaining means wrapping one exception inside another exception while preserving the original cause.

In this program:
- Original exception: IOException
- Wrapped exception: SubmissionStorageException
- Original cause message: Disk quota exceeded
The following code demonstrates the full chained stack trace:
catch (ExamException e) {
    System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());

    System.out.println("Cause: "
            + e.getCause().getMessage());

    System.out.println("Full chained stack trace:");
    e.printStackTrace();
}


The important output is:
Error: Could not store answer02.pdf
Cause: Disk quota exceeded


The stack trace will also show the SubmissionStorageException and its original IOException cause. Exact line numbers depend on where the code is saved.
What happens if the cause is not passed?
Suppose the statement is changed to:
throw new SubmissionStorageException(
    "Could not store " + fileName, null);


Then:
e.getCause()


returns null.

If the program executes:
e.getCause().getMessage()


it throws a NullPointerException because no original cause was preserved.

If the exception is instead re-thrown using only a message:
throw new SubmissionStorageException(
    "Could not store " + fileName, null);


the original IOException details, such as Disk quota exceeded, are lost from the exception chain.
